[06:58.31]Passage One
[07:00.30]Recent AI developments have led to the creation of a series of tools that permit robots to lead different human activities. 
[07:08.20]These tools belong to a group of systems known as ¡°generative AI.¡± 
[07:12.80]The tools use machine learning methods to train AI systems on huge amounts of data to produce human-quality results. 
[07:20.06]One of the most highly publicized generative AI tools is called ChatGPT. 
[07:25.33]It received wide attention by demonstrating the ability to quickly produce written answers to questions at a level and quality similar to humans. 
[07:33.68]The development of generative AI systems has led to some criticism of the technology. 
[07:39.06]Critics have warned that such systems, if used incorrectly, could cause economic, cultural and social harms.
[07:46.53]A Swiss radio station recently carried out a social experiment on air, testing robot-created content and voicing. 
[07:55.59]Station officials said it took three months to train the AI to understand the needs of the station and learn the special requirements of its programming. 
[08:04.72]The experiment lasted 13 hours. 
[08:07.57]The station said in a statement that it received hundreds of messages on the day of the experiment, with some supporting and others opposing. 
[08:16.40]The station's chief defended the project as a lesson on how to live with AI, saying ¡°AI is coming, whether we like it or not. 
[08:24.64]We want to master the technology so we can then put limits on it.¡± 
[08:28.98]He added that about 90 percent of listener reactions suggested the experiment was a good idea. 
[08:35.07]But many said they found the human element missing.
[08:39.94]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[08:45.27]9. What is necessary for generative AI systems to work?
