[09:40.08]Passage Two
[09:41.33]Some important tree species native to the northwestern states in the US are no longer growing well because of climate change. 
[09:48.47]As conditions change in states such as Washington, Oregon and California, trees such as redwood may need to move¡ªwith the help of humans. 
[09:56.94]Some of the trees do not do well when temperatures rise. 
[10:00.27]Others suffer during times of drought. 
[10:02.73]The goal is to move the trees to areas where the climate might be more suitable.
[10:07.15]There are three ways to move trees: assisted population migration, assisted species migration and range expansion. 
[10:14.44]Assisted population migration involves scientists moving a tree's seeds within its current growing range.
[10:21.07]Assisted species migration involves scientists moving a species far from its existing area. 
[10:26.78]The third way, range expansion, moves trees just outside their current growing range. 
[10:31.79]Each way has different results for the trees and other animals and insects that depend on the trees.
[10:38.01]Michael Case is a forest ecologist at the Nature Conservancy in Virginia. 
[10:42.59]He said ¡°there is a huge difference¡± among the three migration methods. 
[10:47.20]His organization believes that assisted population migration has fewer risks. 
[10:51.75]Mr. Case and his team are testing whether the trees, originally from drier parts of the northwest, can do well in western Washington where there is a drought. 
[11:00.70]However, other ecologists and botanists are not sure which is the best way to help. 
[11:05.38]Robert Slesak, who oversees population migration sites run by the US Forest Service in California, Oregon and Washington, said, ¡°Everyone knows we need to do some kind of action related to climate, but there's a real risk of making it worse.¡±
[11:20.49]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[11:25.63]12.Why do Americans move some important tree species from northwestern states to other areas?
