[19:53.46]Recording Three
[19:55.47]Drinking a glass or more of 100% fruit juice each day was linked to a small increase in weight in children and adults, according to a new analysis of prior studies.
[20:05.67] ¡°One fundamental problem with juice is quantity; consuming fruits this way makes it so easy to overdose,¡± said co-author and leading nutrition researcher Dr. Walter Willett, a professor of medicine at Harvard Medical School in Boston. 
[20:20.22]¡°For example, how often do we eat three oranges? 
[20:23.88]Yet, a glass of orange juice is about three oranges that can be consumed in a minute or two, and we can go back and have another, and that will add many calories,¡± Willett said in an email. 
[20:35.17]Over time, too much sugar in the blood can lead to diabetes, heart disease, obesity and other chronic conditions, experts say.
[20:44.07]Due to concerns over the rising rate of childhood obesity and dental cavities, the American Academy of Pediatrics advises parents and guardians to avoid juice entirely for babies younger than 1 year old, limit intake to 4 ounces a day for children ages 1 to 3 years old and only 6 ounces a day for children ages 4 to 6 years old. 
[21:06.55]Teens and adults should drink no more than 8 ounces a day of 100% juice, according to national nutritional guidelines, and juice should not be seen as a healthy way to satisfy thirst. 
[21:19.07]According to Hannon, director of the pediatric diabetes program at Riley Hospital for Children, there is really no health reason to have juice instead of whole fruits and vegetables unless your child cannot tolerate eating regular food.
[21:33.23]For some, the concern over 100% fruit juice may be baffling¡ªfruit is healthy, right? 
[21:39.79]So, what's the difference between the fruit and its juice? 
[21:43.34]Whole fruits and vegetables come in packages of nutrients¡ªcarbohydrates, protein, fat, minerals and vitamins that are all contained with fiber.  
[21:53.58]When we take away the package, we take away the fiber and the structural parts of the food, and our body digests and metabolizes it differently than it evolved to do so. 
[22:04.53]And when you consume calories in solid form, your brain is better at registering those calories and adjusting your food intake accordingly. 
[22:12.95]But if you drink those calories, you may not feel full and begin to eat again.
